>> title: Tonkotsu Broth, Instant Pot
>> category: Soups
>> tags: pork, japanese, stock, ramen, instant pot, pressure cooker
>> counters: Instant Pot
>> aka: instant pot tonkotsu, pressure cooker tonkotsu, pressure cooker ramen broth, tonkotsu in the instant pot, 豚骨, pork bone broth, quick tonkotsu
>> servings: 8
>> dish: tonkotsu-broth
>> kit: Instant Pot
>> pairs-with: tonkotsu-ramen
>> time: 3 hr 30 min
>> step.1: The pot does the extraction. It cannot do the emulsion, and the last twenty minutes with the lid off are where the white comes from.
>> step.2: parboil 30 min, then scrub every bone
>> step.3: cook at high pressure 90 min
>> step.4: wait out the natural release 30 min, aromatics in
>> step.5: boil hard, lid off, 20 min — this is the colour
>> step.6: strain, pressing the softened marrow through

The pot does the extraction and does it in an afternoon instead of a day. What it cannot do is the emulsion: a sealed vessel holds the liquid still, and still is the one thing tonkotsu is never allowed to be. So this is not eight hours made faster — it is ninety minutes of pressure to take the bones apart, and then twenty minutes of the hardest boil your burner will give, uncovered, which is where the broth turns white. Skip the last step and you have a clear, thin, grey pork stock.

Cover @pork trotters{2%lb}(900 g; split lengthways by the butcher), @pork neck bones{2%lb}(900 g) and @pork fatback{6%oz}(170 g) with @cold water{3%qt}(2.8 L) in the #Instant Pot{}, bring to a hard boil on Sauté and ~parboil{30%min}, then tip the lot into the sink and scrub every bone under the tap until nothing brown or grey clings to it. Scrub the inner pot too. Under a lid there is no skimming later, so this is the only chance to take the scum out at all.

Return @&(~1)scrubbed bones{} to the pot with @water{2 1/2%qt}(2.4 L), lock the lid and ~pressure cook{90%min} at high pressure. Less water than the stovetop version on purpose: nothing evaporates under pressure, so the eight-hour recipe's six quarts would come out thin here. Keep the pot no more than two thirds full whatever size it is.

Let @&(~1)pot{} ~natural release{30%min} until the pin drops on its own, then drop @yellow onion{1%large}(halved), @garlic{1%head}(halved across), @fresh ginger{2%in}(sliced) and @scallion greens{4%stalks} into the broth. They go in at the end, not under pressure — ninety sealed minutes boils them to nothing and leaves the same thin vegetal sweetness the stovetop version gets from putting them in early.

Boil @&(~1)broth{} on the highest Sauté setting with the lid off, ~boil{20%min}, until it turns opaque and the fat stops pooling on the surface. This is mechanical, not a matter of time: the boil beats the fat and the dissolved collagen into each other, and until you see it go from tea-coloured to milk-coloured it is not done. If your pot's Sauté will not hold a rolling boil, move the lot to a wide pan on the stove and do it there.

Strain @&(~1)broth{} through a coarse sieve, pressing the softened marrow and knuckle cartilage through with a ladle. It should coat a spoon hot and set to a firm jelly cold. Against the eight-hour pot it is a shade less deep and a shade less sweet; it is white, and it happened today.
